          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-1cfaf0f75c6018e9\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Pierre’s “Je vous aime” directly presents his love for Hélène as true, with his immediately preceding thought independently supporting its avowal force. In this betrothal setting it also weakly to moderately ratifies the expected relationship and impending marriage, although the commitment has largely been socially settled already. The wording is consciously retrieved as the formula appropriate to the occasion, so its emotional awkwardness and exclamation mark do not provide expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is how much undertaking force belongs to the utterance itself: it participates in a betrothal ritual, but Pierre’s commitment has effectively been imposed and publicly recognized before he speaks.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Pierre directly says “Je vous aime” to Hélène during the socially orchestrated culmination of their courtship and betrothal.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Pierre feels carried across a threshold by social pressure and Prince Vasili’s premature treatment of the marriage as settled. He tells himself that he loves Hélène, then produces the expected declaration from conscious recollection of what one says on such occasions. Its feeble sound and his shame expose his uncertainty and alienation from the conventional script without changing the utterance’s basic avowal force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je vous aime!” Pierre says directly to Hélène.\",\"supports\":\"A direct, affirmative avowal of a present loving state, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Pierre thinks: “Now it is too late, it is all over; and besides, I love her.”\",\"supports\":\"Independently confirms that the spoken words present a state Pierre is currently attributing to himself, while also showing his sense of being trapped by events.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He speaks while “remembering what had to be said on these occasions.”\",\"supports\":\"Shows deliberate retrieval of a courtship or betrothal formula, supporting some ritual-relational P force while strongly disfavoring a reflex-like E reading.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Prince Vasili has already blessed the pair, Pierre holds “his fiancée’s” hand, and the marriage follows a month and a half later.\",\"supports\":\"Places the declaration within an impending marital undertaking, supporting moderate P, though the relationship has largely been settled before the declaration.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The words sound so feeble that Pierre feels ashamed of himself.\",\"supports\":\"Indicates awkward and emotionally inadequate delivery, not expressive discharge; it does not undermine the utterance’s T mode.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal, and P captures the limited betrothal-ratifying function. The explicitly recalled formula and feeble delivery are contextual qualifications adequately represented without E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
